{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לא תבערו אש", :translit "lo t'va'aru eish", :gloss "you shall not kindle fire"} {:he "אור שלי", :translit "or sheli", :gloss "my light"} {:he "של גיהנם", :translit "shel gehinom", :gloss "of Gehenna"} {:he "שובת בשבילכם", :translit "shoveit bishvilechem", :gloss "rests for your sake"} {:he "אור שלכם יהא שובת", :translit "or shelachem y'hei shoveit", :gloss "your light too shall rest"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: the verse 'you shall not kindle fire' is read as God suspending both the fire of Gehenna and your kindling on Shabbat."}], :frame {:text "The school of Rabbi Yanninus taught in Shabbat 63b that the prohibition of kindling fire on Shabbat is stated separately, signifying that violating it is treated as a distinct laborage. Genesis 15:17 describes the smoking furnace and flaming torch, imagery the midrashic tradition links to the fires of Gehenna. The Ba'al HaTurim compresses the homiletic reading that on Shabbat the fires of Gehenna themselves rest, as in Shabbat 33b where Gehenna has no respite except on Shabbat.", :sources ["Shabbat 63b" "Shabbat 33b" "Genesis 15:17"]}} {:quotes [{:he "השבת, ויאמר", :translit "ha-shabbat, va-yomer", :gloss "the Sabbath, and He said"} {:he "ר\"ת וס\"ת תורה", :translit "ra'shei teivot va-sofei teivot torah", :gloss "initial letters and final letters — Torah"} {:he "שעיקר התורה בשבת", :translit "she-ikar ha-torah ba-shabbat", :gloss "for the essence of Torah is on Shabbat"} {:he "כשאדם פנוי מעסקיו", :translit "k'she-adam p'nui me'asakav", :gloss "when a person is free from his labors"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "השבת ויאמר", :spells "הו"} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "השבת ויאמר", :spells "תר"}], :frame {:text "The letters assembled from the first and last letters of 'השבת ויאמר' (from Exodus 35:2–3) spell 'תורה,' compressing the rabbinic principle that Shabbat is the time for Torah study. Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael on Exodus 31:13 states that Shabbat was given for Torah study. Tractate Shabbat 113b discusses what activities are permitted on Shabbat, and the Sifrei on Deuteronomy 6:7 connects Torah study to times when one is free from labor.", :sources ["Exodus 35:2" "Exodus 35:3" "Shabbat 113b" "Sifrei Devarim 33"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: the verse 'you shall not kindle fire' is read as God suspending both the fire of Gehenna and your kindling on Shabbat.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "השבת ויאמר", :spells "הו", :letters ["ה" "ו"], :by :selah, :order :in-order} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "השבת ויאמר", :spells "תר", :letters ["ת" "ר"], :by :selah, :order :in-order}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:21:10.276984248Z", :model "glm-5.2"}